The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” I read that as: if a code fence contains any top-level JSON objects, the answer must be the last such object inside that fence, even if the fence contains more than one object and even if a later object appears outside the fence.

The two reply texts are, in full, first: '```json\n{"a":1} {"b":2}\n```\n{"x":0}' and second: '```json\n{"a":1} {"c":3}\n```\n{"x":0}'.

For the first reply, the rule requires the answer to be the object {"b":2}, because it is the last top-level object inside the code fence. For the second reply, the rule requires the answer to be the object {"c":3}, for the same reason. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: it returns {"x":0}. This happens because the implementation treats the entire fenced content as a single candidate; when that candidate is not one well-formed object, it is passed over, and the function then falls through to the top-level objects in the whole text, where the last one is the outside {"x":0} in both replies.

What would make me wrong is the observation that recover_json_object returns {"b":2} for the first text or {"c":3} for the second, or that the two calls return different values. It would also be wrong if the documented rule were read to mean that a fence “holds one” only when its entire content is a single object, in which case the outside object would be the correct answer under the rule.